  "query_preview": "..An impressive early season trough is currently\npositioned across the Pacific Northwest and is producing a notable\nthermal gradient with high temperatures across Utah in the upper\n80s to low 90s while areas to our northwest in southwestern Idaho\nstruggled to get out of the 50s. This thermal gradient is also\ncoinciding with a pressure gradient that has kept winds gusty out\nof the southwest across much of the area with max wind gusts\nbetween 30-40 mph with a few isolated locations between 45-55 mph\n(briefly). This trough will continue to linger over the next\ncouple of days just to our northwest which will keep winds\nelevated and similar in magnitude to what we have experienced the\npast couple of days.\n\nThe base of this trough will rotate through the Great Basin on\nSunday while pulling in tropical moisture from Hurricane Marie.\nThis moisture is slated to arrive Sunday morning in tandem with\na 110+ kt jet. The majority of the guidance has Utah positioned in\nthe right entrance region of this jet leading to upper-level\ndiffluence and a narrow swatch of relatively heavy precipitation.\nThere are some differences in the solution, however with much\ndrier solutions. The 10th-90th percentile QPF totals range from\nalmost nothing to upwards of 1.00\"+ of precipitation from a band\nthat sets up from southwest Utah up through southwestern Wyoming.\nAdditionally, the arrival of a cold front late Sunday into Monday\nmorning will provide another area of focused convection with the\nmajority of guidance pinpointing northwestern Utah as the favored\nlocation of this convection. A high shear/low cape environment is\ndenoted amongst most guidance which could lead to some quasi-\nlinear convection with the potential for strong gusty winds.\n\nThe post-frontal airmass will bring cooler and drier weather into\nthe region through the first half of the week. Beyond that,\nensemble members become divergent with a 45/55 split of a wet/dry\npattern. The 45% of members phase the remaining moisture of\nHurricane Marie into a trough across the PNW with a trajectory\ninto the Great Basin. The other 55% of members phase this moisture\ninto a low off the PNW coast which would lead to a drier solution\nfor the Great Basin.",
